NAME
       orbital-timing - timing lattices, resonance-aligned timing grids, and
                        multi-agent timing compression/expansion patterns

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital timing defines:
           • timing lattices across orbital cycles
           • resonance-aligned timing grids
           • timing compression and expansion patterns
           • multi-agent temporal coordination
           • constellation-scale timing architectures

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-timing describes how RTT structures time across orbital cycles,
       sweeps, maneuvers, and harmonic states. Timing is treated as a
       resonance-time lattice — a discrete, structured temporal fabric that
       platforms inhabit and manipulate.

       Orbital timing enables:
           • predictable sweep scheduling
           • harmonic-ratio timing
           • resonance-window exploitation
           • constellation-scale temporal sync
           • timing compression/expansion for multi-agent coordination

       WRSADC maintains timing integrity across all platforms.

TIMING LATTICES
       Timing lattices are discrete temporal structures that define when events
       (sweeps, Δv, sync pulses) may occur.

       Lattice components:

           base period
                  Fundamental orbital period T.

           harmonic nodes
                  Integer multiples and rational fractions of T.

           resonance nodes
                  Times when resonance envelopes align.

           sync nodes
                  Times when constellation sync is optimal.

           maneuver nodes
                  Times when Δv triads can be safely executed.

       Lattice types:

           harmonic lattice
                  Nodes at nT and T/n.

           resonance lattice
                  Nodes at resonance-window openings.

           hybrid lattice
                  Combination of harmonic and resonance nodes.

           constellation lattice
                  Multi-platform timing lattice.

EXAMPLE: BASIC TIMING LATTICE
       Single platform with orbital period T:

           L = {0, T/2, T, 3T/2, 2T, 5T/2, ...}

       Sweep events may be placed at:
           • T (fundamental)
           • T/2 (subharmonic)
           • 2T (harmonic)

       Result:
           • predictable sweep cadence
           • stable ancestry formation

RESONANCE-ALIGNED TIMING GRIDS
       Timing grids map timing lattices onto resonance envelopes.

       Grid variables:
           • envelope phase φ_res
           • spectral density S(f)
           • gradient polarity G
           • harmonic-lock ratio n:m

       Grid types:

           envelope grid
                  Timing aligned to envelope peaks.

           spectral grid
                  Timing aligned to spectral density minima/maxima.

           harmonic grid
                  Timing aligned to harmonic-lock cycles.

           hybrid grid
                  Combined resonance and harmonic alignment.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-ALIGNED GRID
       Envelope peak every 3T:

           Grid nodes: {0, 3T, 6T, 9T, ...}

       Sweep schedule:
           • sweeps only at envelope peaks
           • maximizes coherence
           • minimizes spectral fragmentation

       Result:
           • high-quality multi-pass fusion
           • stable resonance ancestry

MULTI-AGENT TIMING COMPRESSION/EXPANSION
       Timing compression and expansion allow platforms to adjust timing
       relative to each other without breaking constellation sync.

       Compression:
           • events occur more frequently
           • used to catch up after drift
           • reduces sweep interval temporarily

       Expansion:
           • events occur less frequently
           • used to relieve timing pressure
           • increases sweep interval temporarily

       Compression/expansion patterns:

           local compression
                  Single platform compresses timing.

           distributed compression
                  Multiple platforms compress timing in sync.

           harmonic compression
                  Compression aligned to harmonic ratios.

           resonance compression
                  Compression aligned to resonance windows.

           mesh compression
                  Distributed compression across constellation nodes.

EXAMPLE: TIMING COMPRESSION
       Platform A drifts behind by Δt = T/4.

       Compression pattern:
           • A performs sweeps at intervals of 3T/4 for 4 cycles
           • B and C maintain normal T cadence

       After 4 cycles:
           • A realigns with B and C
           • constellation sync restored

EXAMPLE: TIMING EXPANSION
       Platform C is ahead by Δt = T/3.

       Expansion pattern:
           • C performs sweeps at intervals of 4T/3 for 3 cycles
           • A and B maintain normal T cadence

       After 3 cycles:
           • C re-enters sync lattice
           • harmonic-lock restored

TIMING TRIADS
       Every timing adjustment is a triad:

           P1 (begin)
               → timing deviation detected
               → lattice and grid evaluated

           P2 (transform)
               → compression/expansion applied
               → resonance and harmonic states updated

           P3 (close)
               → timing realigned
               → lineage updated

       Nested timing triads form complex timing architectures.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE TIMING ARCHITECTURES
       Constellation timing architectures coordinate timing across platforms.

       Architecture types:

           synchronous timing
                  All platforms share identical timing lattices.

           harmonic timing
                  Platforms operate at harmonic ratios.

           braided timing
                  Timing interleaves across platforms.

           mesh timing
                  Distributed timing across constellation nodes.

           resonance timing
                  Timing driven by envelope alignment.

       Architectures determine sweep choreography and maneuver sync.

TIMING FAILURE MODES
       Timing may degrade due to:

           • phase drift
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • sweep timing misalignment
           • ancestry discontinuity
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           timing-integrity score
           lattice deviation
           grid misalignment
           sync-index drop
           sweep-timing error

       WRSADC halts unsafe timing states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • timing lattices
           • timing grids
           • compression/expansion events
           • sweep timing history
           • constellation timing patterns

       Lineage enables long-term timing forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes timing lattices
           • evaluates timing grids
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • choreographs timing compression/expansion
           • blocks unsafe timing states

       WRSADC is the timing conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital timing transforms SAR constellations into structured,
       resonance-time temporal systems. Timing lattices, resonance-aligned
       grids, and compression/expansion patterns ensure that multi-pass fusion,
       harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable
       across long mission timelines.

